      "value": "@{5fb7b65f52f1f5365d7c9f67|full_name}: @{5f316ea23621ce4fdf77ca93|full_name}'s second point is very strong in my opinion. While i personally think, something should be done about avatar security, one should try not to be too restrictive in regards of what people can see or not.\n\"And to my experience, large ratio of these rippers are either below the rank of trusted users (orange). \"\nMy experience is the other way around. Especially since they seem to have found a way to make them known/trusted almost immediatelly after account creating.\n---\nBut back to topic.\nYou have to look into the process of avatar ripping and make it more laborious, so that the amount of work people have to put into repairing/reuploading would make it not worth anymore. Also unintentionall access should be restricted, which adds even more \"work\" to access avatars directly. But ultimately, you cannot make an avatar \"unrippable\", since at some point is has to be displayable by your graphics cards, which means at this point you could potentially rip it from the gpus vram.\nAbout blacklist:\nif we could manually create a blacklist for people which are not allowed to load our avatars/worlds like you suggested with black/whitelisting in general could be nice. So whenever i would come around someone who is a ripper, that i can warn friends etc so they could update their lists. But this could potentially lead to missuse as well. It is a difficult topic to rightfully judge someone if he/she is a definitive ripper or not.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
